window.COURSE_DATA={"phases":[{"id":1,"name":"Molecular foundations","weeks":[1,7],"goal":"Close the biology gap at the level an engineer needs: the chemistry of the molecules, the catalysis they perform, and the information system that produces them. Nothing here is appreciation — every topic returns later as a variable in a model or a target in a design."},{"id":2,"name":"Metabolism in depth","weeks":[8,13],"goal":"Learn central metabolism as an accounting system rather than a set of maps. Every pathway is studied for its carbon, electron and ATP ledger, because those ledgers are what you will later manipulate."},{"id":3,"name":"Quantitative physiology","weeks":[14,18],"goal":"Biomass becomes a product with a formula, growth becomes a rate equation, and a fermentation becomes a balance you can close. This is where your degree starts paying for itself."},{"id":4,"name":"Constraint-based modelling","weeks":[19,26],"goal":"Build, interrogate and criticise genome-scale models. Flux balance analysis is a steady-state mass balance with a linear program on top; you already know both halves, so the work is in the biology of the constraints and the honesty of the interpretation."},{"id":5,"name":"Measurement and control of flux","weeks":[27,31],"goal":"What a model predicts against what a cell did. Labelling, metabolomics, kinetic models and control analysis — the four things that separate a flux claim from a flux opinion."},{"id":6,"name":"The molecular toolbox","weeks":[32,39],"goal":"Every build-step tool: what it does, what it costs in days, what it fails at. You will not get bench hours from a book, so the aim is fluency plus a written bench plan precise enough that a supervisor could hand it to a technician."},{"id":7,"name":"Hosts, products and the literature","weeks":[40,46],"goal":"The canon. By the end you can name, for every major host and product class, what has been achieved, at what titre, by whom, and what stopped it going further."},{"id":8,"name":"Process, economics and the application","weeks":[47,55],"goal":"Reaction engineering for immobilised and heterogeneous systems, then the strain inside a real process and a real economy, and finally fifty-five weeks converted into a proposal, a portfolio and a shortlist. This phase is where a chemical engineer out-argues a pure biologist, so it is worth the nine weeks."}],"weeks":[{"n":1,"p":1,"t":"The cell as a chemical plant","obj":["Name every compartment of E. coli and S. cerevisiae where a heterologous pathway could sit, and the transport problem each creates","Quote from memory the orders of magnitude of cell volume, protein copy number, metabolite concentration and doubling time","State the central dogma and identify each step as an engineering handle","Justify why four or five organisms dominate industrial practice"],"s":["Read Microbiology 3.3 on prokaryotic cell structure. Draw E. coli from memory afterwards, then correct it in a second colour.","Read Microbiology 3.4 and Alberts Ch 1. Draw S. cerevisiae the same way and label every compartment a pathway could occupy.","Read Shuler Ch 1-2. Start glossary.md: every term you did not know, with the page you met it on.","Numbers session: tabulate cell volume, dry weight, protein content, ribosome count and doubling time for E. coli, S. cerevisiae and C. glutamicum.","Derive the three conversions you will use all year: molecules per cell to molar, mmol gDW⁻¹ h⁻¹ to molecules per cell per second, and g/L to C-mol/L.","Read Berg Ch 1 and write one page on why the double helix is 'an expression of the rules of chemistry'.","Read Lee Ch 1 §1.1. Note the three eras of the field, who drove each, and what technology made each possible.","Read Lee §1.4-1.6. Build a host table: native strengths, tool maturity, industrial products, regulatory status.","Install and configure Python, Jupyter, git and a reference manager. Pin every dependency in a lock file as well as a loose specification, and record the Python and solver versions.","Build the repository: README, /notebooks, /models, /design, /notes, /process, /bench, /proposal, glossary.md, log.md, plus a Makefile with setup, test and all targets and a data/PROVENANCE.md skeleton.","Write the README from the template in Methods §8.2 — question, answer, what it does not show — then run the clean-clone test on an empty directory and fix whatever breaks.","Weekly review: reread your notes, list the three things you understood least, and take the first recall set."]},{"n":2,"p":1,"t":"Water, bonds and the chemistry of the cytosol","obj":["Compute buffer composition and pH shifts for a fermentation and for an intracellular compartment","Explain how the four weak interactions determine structure and specificity","Relate hydrophobicity and pKa to membrane partitioning and product toxicity","Account for the effect of undissociated acid on a fermenting cell"],"s":["Read Berg §1.3 and write the energies of the four weak interactions with one biological example each.","Derive Henderson-Hasselbalch from the dissociation equilibrium, without looking.","Problems: compute the composition of a phosphate buffer at pH 7.0 and its capacity; repeat for citrate.","Read Microbiology 9.3. Write what intracellular pH homeostasis costs in ATP, and how acidophiles differ.","Derive the undissociated-acid mechanism: for lactic acid at pH 4.5 and pH 6.0, compute the undissociated fraction and explain the titre consequence.","Compute the proton flux a cell must pump to hold internal pH at 7.5 in a medium at pH 5.0, and the ATP cost.","Read on the hydrophobic effect; compute log P for butanol, hexanol and octanol and rank their membrane toxicity.","Write the toxicity note: three mechanisms by which a weak-acid or alcohol product limits its own titre.","Code a small pH and speciation calculator for the acids you care about; commit it.","List the five environmental variables a fermenter controls and why — Stanbury Ch 2 is growth kinetics and does not cover them; use Ch 8 and Ch 9, or Shuler Ch 9, when those books are to hand.","Write the week summary into notes/: cytosol chemistry and what it constrains.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":3,"p":1,"t":"Protein structure and folding","obj":["Draw all twenty side chains grouped by chemistry and predict their behaviour in a fold","Explain the four levels of structure and which interactions hold each","Account for misfolding, inclusion bodies and the chaperone system","Read a PDB entry and an AlphaFold model critically"],"s":["Read Berg Ch 2 §2.1-2.2 and draw all twenty side chains grouped by chemistry. Keep the sheet.","Read §2.3-2.4 on secondary structure; build a helix and a sheet from the hydrogen-bonding pattern on paper.","Read §2.5-2.6 on tertiary and quaternary structure; write what each interaction contributes to stability.","Folding session: write the thermodynamics of folding, the magnitude of ΔG(folding), and why it is small.","Read on inclusion bodies; write the three standard remedies and the mechanism each exploits.","Read Berg Ch 3 §3.1 — purification by solubility, size, charge and affinity, and separation by gel electrophoresis — this is how every protein measurement later is made.","Read Berg Ch 3 §3.4; write the workflow from cell lysate to a proteomic data table.","Structure session: open one enzyme in a viewer, identify the active site, and list every residue within 5 Å of the substrate.","Compare the crystal structure with the AlphaFold model of the same protein; note where they differ and what that means.","Write enzyme-dossier.md part one: sequence, structure, fold family, subunit composition, cofactors.","Read Protein Engineering Ch 6 framing; write what a sequence model can and cannot tell you about this enzyme.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":4,"p":1,"t":"Enzyme catalysis: how the rate is actually achieved","obj":["Explain transition-state stabilisation and compute the rate enhancement it buys","Describe the main catalytic strategies with their canonical examples","Relate cofactor chemistry to pathway-level cofactor demand","Interpret an EC number and predict a reaction's cofactor requirement from it"],"s":["Read Berg §8.1-8.3 and derive the relation between ΔG‡ and rate; compute the rate enhancement for a 30 kJ/mol reduction.","Read Berg Ch 9 §9.1 on proteases; write the full catalytic mechanism of chymotrypsin with arrows.","Read Berg §9.2 on carbonic anhydrases; write why a zinc ion lowers the pKa of bound water.","Read Berg §9.3 on restriction enzymes with the chapter opener on basic catalytic principles and Figure 8.9; write what induced fit achieves kinetically. NMP kinases are not a section in the 7th edition — §9.4 is myosins.","Cofactor session: tabulate NAD, NADP, FAD, CoA, TPP, PLP, biotin and heme with the chemistry each enables.","Write the cofactor-demand note: for a pathway of your choice, total the cofactor requirement per mole of product.","EC session: take ten enzymes from a pathway and classify them; predict the cofactor from the class and check.","BRENDA session: for your dossier enzyme, record kcat, KM, pH and temperature optima, inhibitors and the assay conditions of each.","Compare three independent kcat reports for the same enzyme; write four mechanistic explanations of the spread.","Read Microbiology 8.1 and write how enzyme activity is regulated at four distinct levels.","Extend enzyme-dossier.md: mechanism, cofactor, kinetic parameters with uncertainty, and which value you would model.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":5,"p":1,"t":"Enzyme kinetics I: your kinetics, re-pointed","obj":["Derive Michaelis-Menten from the QSSA and from rapid equilibrium, and state where each holds","Fit kinetic parameters by weighted non-linear regression with confidence intervals","Discriminate the four inhibition mechanisms from data","Judge which parameters are identifiable from a given experiment"],"s":["Read Berg §8.4 and derive Michaelis-Menten from the QSSA on paper, from scratch.","Derive it again by rapid equilibrium; state the condition under which the two KM differ.","Read Shuler Ch 3 §3.1-3.3 and work every solved example by hand before reading its solution.","Derive the reversible rate law and the Haldane relation; explain why irreversible kinetics is wrong in central metabolism.","Read Berg §8.5 on inhibition; derive the rate law for each of the four mechanisms.","Code the fitting notebook: simulate noisy v-S data, fit by Lineweaver-Burk and by weighted non-linear least squares.","Quantify the bias: show numerically how much the linearised fit misestimates Vmax and KM, and explain why.","Extend to inhibition: fit all four mechanisms to one data set and discriminate with an information criterion.","Identifiability study: show which parameters a single substrate titration can and cannot resolve.","Read Shuler Ch 3 §3.4 on immobilized enzyme systems; derive the effectiveness factor and connect it to the Thiele modulus you already know.","Read Lee §5.2-5.3; write five reasons an in vitro kcat does not apply in vivo, with a magnitude for each.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":6,"p":1,"t":"Enzyme kinetics II: allostery and regulation","obj":["Explain cooperativity and fit the Hill equation without over-interpreting n","Compare the MWC and KNF models and state what data distinguishes them","Describe covalent modification cascades and zero-order ultrasensitivity","Identify feedback inhibition in a pathway and propose a feedback-resistant design"],"s":["Read Berg Ch 7 and write why cooperative oxygen binding is physiologically necessary, with the numbers.","Derive the Hill equation; state precisely what n is and what it is not.","Read Berg Ch 10 §10.1 on aspartate transcarbamoylase; write the allosteric logic including the effect of CTP and ATP.","Read the MWC model; derive the fraction of R state as a function of ligand and the allosteric constant L.","Compare MWC with KNF; write the experiment that would distinguish them for your dossier enzyme.","Read Berg §10.3 on covalent modification; derive zero-order ultrasensitivity and explain its switch-like behaviour.","Read Palsson §2.4 and reproduce the PFK bound-state reconstruction, counting every state and reaction.","Code the allostery notebook: simulate MWC kinetics, show how L and the effector concentrations shift the curve.","Add the feedback loop: a two-step pathway with end-product inhibition of step one; show the steady-state regulation it produces.","Read Berg §16.2; list every allosteric effector of PFK and pyruvate kinase.","Write the regulation note: for one biosynthetic pathway, which feedback you would remove first and what you expect to break.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":7,"p":1,"t":"Nucleic acids, the central dogma, and consolidation","obj":["Write the chemistry of replication, transcription and translation with the enzymes named","Explain the genetic code, codon bias and the consequences for heterologous expression","Describe operons, polycistronic transcripts and the prokaryote-eukaryote differences that constrain host choice","Sit and mark Paper 1 honestly"],"s":["Read Berg Ch 4 end to end; write the phosphodiester and peptide bond chemistry from memory.","Read Alberts Ch 6 — Basic Genetic Mechanisms, named section \"DNA Replication\"; draw the fork with every enzyme named.","Read Berg Ch 29; draw bacterial transcription initiation, elongation and both termination mechanisms.","Read Berg Ch 30; write the translation cycle and state where initiation rate is set.","Codon session: pull a gene from NCBI, compute codon adaptation against the E. coli table, and plot the ten worst codons.","Read Microbiology Ch 10 and answer its review questions in writing.","Write the expression note: six mechanistically distinct reasons a heterologous gene expresses poorly.","Consolidation: reread all six weeks of notes and compress them into a four-page summary.","Gap session: take your three weakest topics from the review lists and reread the primary text on each.","Sit Paper 1 under exam conditions: two and a half hours, closed book, on paper.","Mark Paper 1 against the scheme. Record the score and write what each lost mark revealed.","Weekly review, recall set, and plan any remediation into the coming weeks."]},{"n":8,"p":2,"t":"Bioenergetics: ATP, redox carriers and the proton motive force","obj":["Compute ΔG′ at physiological concentrations and distinguish it cleanly from ΔG°′ and ΔG°","Explain stoichiometric coupling to ATP hydrolysis without hand-waving","Account for NADH, NADPH, FADH2 and the proton motive force separately","State the P/O ratio, its uncertainty, and what that uncertainty does to a model"],"s":["Read Berg Ch 15 §15.1-15.2; write worked numerical examples distinguishing ΔG°, ΔG°′ and ΔG′.","Read Smith Van Ness §13.1-13.5 and recompute one biochemical equilibrium in the chemical engineering formalism; reconcile with the biochemical answer.","Read Berg §15.3 on activated carriers; tabulate every carrier with the group it transfers.","Derive the phosphoryl transfer potential of ATP and explain the three contributions to it.","Read Berg Ch 18 §18.1-18.3; draw the respiratory chain with proton stoichiometry on every complex.","Read §18.4-18.6 on ATP synthase; write how the c-ring stoichiometry sets the H⁺/ATP ratio.","Compute ATP per glucose aerobically at P/O 2.5/1.5, then at 2.0 and 1.0; tabulate the spread.","Write the sensitivity note: how much predicted biomass yield moves across that P/O range, and why models fit GAM instead.","NADPH session: list every NADPH-generating reaction in E. coli and estimate the maximum NADPH per glucose.","Read on transhydrogenase; write what it converts a carbon problem into, and its energetic cost.","Read Microbiology 8.3; write how anaerobic respiration with alternative acceptors changes the energetics.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":9,"p":2,"t":"Glycolysis and gluconeogenesis","obj":["Write all ten glycolytic reactions with cofactors from memory","Explain the regulatory architecture and the futile cycles","Compare EMP, Entner-Doudoroff and phosphoketolase routes on ATP, NADPH and protein cost","Build the stoichiometric matrix of glycolysis by hand"],"s":["Read Berg §16.1; write all ten reactions with cofactors, then check and correct.","Read §16.2 on the fates of pyruvate and the entry of other sugars; map fructose, galactose and glycerol entry.","Read §16.3-16.4 on regulation; list every allosteric effector and the physiological logic of each.","Read §16.5 on gluconeogenesis; identify the four bypass reactions and the ATP cost of the cycle.","Compute the ATP cost of a futile cycle at a given cycling rate, and say how you would detect one experimentally.","Read Microbiology 8.2; write the Entner-Doudoroff and phosphoketolase stoichiometries.","Compare the three routes quantitatively: ATP, NADH, NADPH and enzyme count per glucose.","Read Palsson §2.1-2.2 and reproduce Table 2.1 and 2.2 by hand.","Build the glycolysis stoichiometric matrix in NumPy; verify every column is elementally and charge balanced.","Compute the null space of your matrix and interpret each basis vector biologically.","Write the glycolysis section of the pathway atlas: map, matrix, ledger of carbon, electrons and ATP.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":10,"p":2,"t":"The pentose phosphate pathway and the NADPH economy","obj":["Write the oxidative and non-oxidative branches with correct stoichiometry","Compute the glycolysis-PPP split required for a given NADPH demand, and the carbon cost","Explain how the non-oxidative branch rearranges carbon, and why it is reversible","Trace R5P and E4P supply to nucleotides and aromatics"],"s":["Read Berg §20.3; write the oxidative branch with its stoichiometry and the carbon released.","Read §20.4; work through the non-oxidative rearrangement on paper until 3 pentose to 2 F6P + GAP is obvious.","Derive all four classical PPP flux modes and the condition that selects each.","Compute the carbon lost as CO₂ for NADPH demands of 4, 8, 12 and 16 per glucose.","Read §20.5 on glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency; write what it reveals about redox buffering.","Read Palsson §2.3 and reproduce the combined glycolysis-PPP matrix from Tables 2.3-2.4.","Extend your NumPy matrix to include the PPP; recompute the null space and interpret the new basis vectors.","Code the split-ratio calculator: given an NADPH demand, return the required PPP flux and the carbon yield penalty.","List four documented cofactor interventions with the yield change each produced — Lee Ch 11 supplies only one, so take the rest from the week 10 NADPH-economy sources.","Write the NADPH economy note: rank the supply routes by carbon cost, ATP cost and engineering difficulty.","Add the PPP to the pathway atlas with its ledger.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":11,"p":2,"t":"The citric acid cycle, anaplerosis and the glyoxylate shunt","obj":["Write the TCA cycle with cofactors and compute its full energetic yield","Name every anaplerotic and cataplerotic reaction in E. coli, yeast and C. glutamicum and the differences between them","Explain the glyoxylate shunt and when it is essential","Quantify the carbon cost of running the oxidative cycle for a C4 product"],"s":["Read Berg §17.1 on pyruvate dehydrogenase; write the three-enzyme mechanism and its cofactors.","Read §17.2; write all eight cycle reactions with cofactors and the two CO₂ release points.","Read §17.3-17.4 on regulation and the cycle's biosynthetic role; list every compound drawn off the cycle.","Compute the full energetic yield of one acetyl-CoA through the cycle under two P/O assumptions.","Map the PEP-pyruvate-oxaloacetate node on one page for E. coli, then annotate yeast and C. glutamicum differences.","Read Lee §12.4.1.2; write why pyruvate carboxylase matters for lysine and for succinate.","Read Berg §17.5; derive why growth on acetate is impossible without it.","Compute both routes to succinate — oxidative and reductive — and their maximum yields from glucose.","Extend the stoichiometric matrix to the full central metabolism of Palsson Figure 2.5; verify balance.","Compute the carbon lost to CO₂ at three TCA flux levels and relate it to biomass demand.","Add the TCA cycle and anaplerosis to the pathway atlas with its ledger.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":12,"p":2,"t":"Respiration, electron transport and the energetics of aeration","obj":["Draw the respiratory chain of E. coli with its branched terminal oxidases","Relate oxygen uptake rate to ATP demand and to carbon flux","Explain anaerobic respiration and the hierarchy of electron acceptors","Compute the specific oxygen demand of a producing strain"],"s":["Draw the E. coli respiratory chain with both NADH dehydrogenases and both terminal oxidases, with H⁺/e⁻ for each.","Compute the effective P/O for each combination of dehydrogenase and oxidase; explain the four-fold range.","Read Microbiology 9.2; write how oxygen requirement classes are determined experimentally.","Read Microbiology 8.4 on anaerobic respiration; rank the acceptors by redox potential and ATP yield.","Derive OTR = kLa(C*−C) and the steady-state balance with OUR. Shuler Ch 6 has NO oxygen-transfer section — confirmed against the PDF; use Shuler §10.2.3 on aeration, agitation and heat transfer (printed p292), or Doran §9.9-9.10 on mass-transfer correlations and the measurement of kLa, which are verified above.","Compute the OUR of a strain at a stated growth rate and productivity, from the ATP and redox balance.","Read Stanbury Ch 9 on the oxygen requirements of industrial fermentations; compute the required kLa for that OUR and compare with realistic vessel capability.","Write the microaerobic note: why some products are made best at low dissolved oxygen, with two examples.","Extend the central metabolism matrix with the respiratory reactions and exchange reactions for O₂ and CO₂.","Compute maximum ATP per glucose from your own matrix by hand-solving the balance; compare with week 8.","Add respiration to the pathway atlas; note the explicit assumptions behind every ATP number in it.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":13,"p":2,"t":"Fermentation routes and redox balancing","obj":["Write the mixed-acid, homolactic, heterolactic and butanediol fermentations with full stoichiometry","Balance electrons across any product spectrum and detect what is missing","Explain why fermentation persists aerobically as overflow metabolism","Consolidate phase 2 into a usable reference"],"s":["Read Microbiology 8.4; write each named fermentation with its full balanced equation.","Derive the mixed-acid product spectrum of E. coli and show that electrons balance, including H₂.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 4; write homolactic and heterolactic equations and compute ATP and mass yield for each.","Read Lee §15.1.2 with §15.3.2.2 on acid tolerance; note what limits lactate titre in practice rather than in theory.","Balance a given experimental product spectrum; find the missing product from the electron gap.","Read on overflow metabolism; write the proteome-efficiency explanation and the evidence for it.","Code the redox-balancing notebook: input a product spectrum, output carbon and electron closure and the implied missing stream.","Extend the matrix with all fermentative routes; compute the anaerobic ATP maximum and the product spectrum that achieves it.","Complete the pathway atlas: all of phase 2 in one document, with a consistent ledger format throughout.","Consolidation: compress phase 2 into a five-page reference you will actually reuse in phase 4.","Gap session: reread the primary text on your three weakest topics from the week reviews.","Weekly review and recall set; plan remediation into phase 3."]},{"n":14,"p":3,"t":"Biosynthesis: amino acids, nucleotides and the precursor tree","obj":["Trace every amino acid and nucleotide back to one of the twelve precursors","Explain the regulation of branched biosynthetic pathways and the role of isozymes","Compute the ATP, NADPH and precursor cost of a named amino acid","Propose a feedback-resistant design for an amino acid strain"],"s":["Read Berg §24.1-24.2 on nitrogen fixation and assimilation; compute the ATP cost of GDH versus GS-GOGAT.","Read §24.3; build the amino acid family tree on one page, rooted at the twelve precursors.","Compute the full cost of lysine from glucose: precursors, NADPH, ATP, and the theoretical yield.","Repeat for phenylalanine via shikimate; note the E4P and PEP demand and what that implies for the PPP.","Read §24.4 on regulation; write the three feedback architectures and an example of each.","Read Lee §12.4.1.2; list the interventions used and the yield achieved at each generation of strain.","Design exercise: propose the feedback-resistant alleles and the flux changes for an aromatic strain.","Read Berg Ch 25 §25.1-25.3; write the de novo purine and pyrimidine routes and their precursor demand.","Read §25.4-25.5 on salvage and regulation; write why salvage matters for a fast-growing production strain.","Code: extend your matrix with the biosynthesis of three amino acids; verify balance and compute their yields.","Write notes/biosynthesis.md: the precursor tree, the cofactor demand table, and the regulation summary.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":15,"p":3,"t":"Lipids, membranes, transport and substrate uptake","obj":["Describe every transport mode with its energy source and stoichiometry","Write the PTS reaction chain and compute its PEP cost","Explain carbon catabolite repression and the problem it creates for mixed feedstocks","Reason quantitatively about product partitioning, toxicity and efflux"],"s":["Read Berg Ch 12 §12.1-12.3; write the thermodynamics of partitioning for a C4, C6 and C8 alcohol.","Read Berg §22.4; write the cycle and compute acetyl-CoA, ATP and NADPH per C16 chain.","Read Berg Ch 13 §13.1-13.3; tabulate the four transport modes with energy source and stoichiometry.","Write the full PTS reaction chain for glucose and derive the PEP cost per glucose taken up.","Compute the yield ceiling for a PEP-derived product with and without the PTS; state when replacement pays.","Read on catabolite repression; write the mechanism and three engineering strategies for xylose co-utilisation.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 3; write how transporter repertoire rather than intracellular enzymes sets substrate scope.","Read Alberts Ch 12 — named sections on compartmentalization, nuclear transport, and import into mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes, with Ch 13 for the vacuole; list the yeast compartments used for pathways and the targeting signal each needs. Cite the prose, not Table 12-3, which does not extract from this PDF.","Toxicity session: for a chosen hydrophobic product, estimate the aqueous concentration at which membrane effects begin.","Write the efflux note: three countermeasures for product toxicity and the measurement that would show each is working.","Extend the matrix with uptake and export reactions; show how transport stoichiometry changes the yield ceiling.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":16,"p":3,"t":"Biomass composition and the biomass equation","obj":["Convert a macromolecular composition into a precursor and energy demand per gram dry weight","Write a biomass equation and check it elementally","Separate growth-associated from non-growth-associated maintenance and obtain both from data","Explain how biomass composition changes with growth rate and what that does to a model"],"s":["Write the empirical formula of E. coli biomass and compute its degree of reduction, from Shuler Ch 7 — section number unconfirmed, so cite the chapter until the PDF is to hand. Week 16's biomass equation is the cross-check.","Take a published macromolecular composition and convert it to mmol of each precursor per gram dry weight, showing the arithmetic.","Compute the polymerisation ATP cost per gram biomass from first principles; compare with published GAM.","Write your own biomass equation and verify it balances elementally; list every assumption you made.","Compare your coefficients with those in a published genome-scale model; explain every discrepancy larger than twenty per cent.","Read Lee §2.5.2.3; write what it means that an ME model computes the BOF rather than assuming it.","Derive the Pirt relation; show how GAM and NGAM are obtained from a chemostat series by regression.","Simulate the regression with realistic noise; show how many dilution rates are needed to resolve NGAM.","Read on growth-rate dependence of composition; quantify the RNA fraction change from 0.1 to 0.8 h⁻¹.","Compute the resulting change in precursor demand and state the consequence for a fixed-BOF model.","Write notes/biomass.md with the full derivation and a defended biomass equation for your host.","Weekly review and recall set — and the first supervisor contact, which belongs here at month four rather than in week 55: one email to one group whose recent paper you have actually read, with one specific sentence about an unresolved result in it. Contact made now redirects which phases you go deep on; contact made in week 55 cannot."]},{"n":17,"p":3,"t":"Stoichiometry, yields and data reconciliation","obj":["Close elemental and available-electron balances on real fermentation data","Compute theoretical maxima by degree of reduction and by linear programming, and reconcile them","Detect and localise gross errors in a measured data set","Separate carbon, mass and molar yield and state which decides which question"],"s":["Work every solved example in Shuler Ch 7 by hand before reading its solution.","Work every end-of-chapter problem in Shuler Ch 7; mark your own answers.","Derive maximum yields of ethanol, lactate, acetate and succinate by degree of reduction alone.","Repeat all four by setting up and solving a small linear program; reconcile any difference in writing.","Derive the succinate case with CO₂ fixation; show where the extra carbon comes from and what pays for it.","Build the yield calculator module: substrate and product formulae in, maximum yields and binding constraint out.","Write unit tests, including ethanol at 0.511 g/g and at least two cofactor-constrained cases.","Reconciliation exercise: take a data set closing to 91% on carbon and localise the error by redundancy analysis.","Code gross error detection: compute the test statistic and show which measurement it implicates.","Write the RQ note: what respiratory quotient tells you in real time and what it cannot distinguish.","Write the thresholds note: for one product, the titre, rate and yield a process would need, with sources for each number.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":18,"p":3,"t":"Growth kinetics and physiological states","obj":["Derive and solve batch, fed-batch and chemostat balances","Fit Monod parameters and demonstrate why they are poorly identifiable from batch data","Design a chemostat series that resolves µmax, Ks, GAM and NGAM","Sit and mark Paper 2"],"s":["Read Shuler Ch 6 §6.1-6.3; work every solved example by hand.","Read Stanbury Ch 2, named sections \"batch culture\" and \"continuous culture\"; derive the chemostat steady state and washout condition.","Derive the dilution rate that maximises biomass productivity and evaluate it for realistic parameters.","Code the batch simulator: Monod ODEs, integrated, with substrate and biomass trajectories.","Fit µmax and Ks to noisy synthetic batch data; plot the joint confidence region and explain the correlation.","Extend to a chemostat; reproduce the washout transition numerically and compare with the analytical criterion.","Extend to fed-batch with exponential feeding; show how the feed profile holds µ at a setpoint.","Derive Luedeking-Piret; compute the growth rate at which product formation becomes mostly non-growth-associated.","Design the experiment: specify a chemostat series that would resolve µmax, Ks, GAM and NGAM, with the number of points justified.","Consolidation: compress phase 3 into a five-page quantitative reference.","Sit Paper 2 under exam conditions: three hours, open notes, on paper.","Mark Paper 2 against the scheme; record the score and write what each lost mark revealed."]},{"n":19,"p":4,"t":"Microbial genetics and genome annotation","obj":["Explain how an annotated genome is produced and where annotation fails","Read a locus tag, an EC assignment and a GO term correctly, and judge the evidence behind each","Describe mutation, recombination and horizontal gene transfer as sources of genetic variation","Find and assess the evidence for one gene's function from primary sources"],"s":["Read Microbiology Ch 11.1-11.4; write how DNA replication fidelity and repair set the mutation rate.","Read Ch 11.5-11.8 on mutation, HGT and conjugation; write what each means for strain stability.","Read Berg Ch 6 §6.1-6.2 on homologs and the statistical analysis of alignments; write the difference between orthologue and paralogue and why it matters for annotation.","Annotation session: take one poorly characterised gene and trace its functional assignment to its original evidence.","Read Palsson §3.1; write what distinguishes a one-dimensional from a two-dimensional annotation.","Database session: for one metabolic gene, pull records from NCBI, UniProt, KEGG and EcoCyc and tabulate the disagreements.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 1; write what genome reduction costs an engineer and what it buys the organism.","Write the annotation-error note: five ways an annotation can be wrong and the consequence of each in a model.","Code: fetch a genome's annotation programmatically and count genes by functional category.","Compute the fraction of genes in your host with experimental rather than inferred evidence; comment.","Write notes/annotation.md summarising how you will judge annotation quality for the rest of the course.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":20,"p":4,"t":"Network reconstruction: the concept and the stoichiometric matrix","obj":["Build a stoichiometric matrix from a pathway by hand and verify it","Explain the six axioms underlying constraint-based reconstruction","Describe how protein states and regulation can be represented stoichiometrically","Distinguish a reconstruction from a model"],"s":["Read Palsson Ch 1 §1.1-1.3; write what a mechanistic genotype-phenotype relationship means operationally.","Read §1.4; write each of the six axioms in your own words with one consequence of each.","Read Palsson Ch 2 §2.1-2.2; reproduce Figure 2.1 and its matrix by hand.","Read §2.3; reproduce the combined glycolysis-PPP matrix and its partitioned structure.","Read §2.4; reproduce the PFK bound-state subnetwork and count its states and reactions.","Read §2.5-2.6; write the difference between the core E. coli network and a genome-scale reconstruction.","Download the E. coli core model; report its dimensions, compartments and exchange reactions.","Verify mass and charge balance on every reaction in the core model programmatically; report any exceptions.","Compare your hand-built central metabolism matrix from phase 2 with the core model; list every difference.","Write the scope note: what your phase 2 matrix omits and what each omission would change.","Inspect iML1515: dimensions, gene count, compartments; compare the scale with the core model.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":21,"p":4,"t":"The reconstruction process: curation, GPRs and gap filling","obj":["Describe the four-step reconstruction process and the data each step needs","Write and interpret GPR associations, including complexes and isozymes","Identify dead ends and blocked reactions and propose gap-filling","Assess a published reconstruction's quality from its documentation"],"s":["Read Palsson §3.2 step by step; write what data each of the four steps consumes and produces.","Read Lee §2.3; write the difference between automated draft and curated reconstruction, with the error types of each.","GPR session: parse the GPR strings of twenty core model reactions and classify each as complex, isozyme or single.","Code: implement gene deletion from GPR logic yourself, without using the library's built-in function.","Verify your implementation against COBRApy's gene deletion for all genes in the core model.","Find every dead-end metabolite and blocked reaction in the core model; explain each biologically.","Read on gap filling; write why an algorithmically filled gap is a hypothesis rather than a fact.","Take one blocked reaction and propose the specific gap-fill, with the evidence you would need to accept it.","Read Palsson §3.3-3.4; write how a reconstruction is used beyond simulation.","Run a quality-control pass on the core model: balance, directionality, duplicate reactions, orphan genes.","Write notes/reconstruction-quality.md: the checklist you will apply to any model you use from now on.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":22,"p":4,"t":"The linear algebra of S: subspaces, null spaces and conserved pools","obj":["Compute and interpret all four fundamental subspaces of a stoichiometric matrix","Explain what the left null space says about conserved moieties","Use singular value decomposition to study network structure","Relate the null space dimension to the number of measurements a flux study needs"],"s":["Compute all four subspaces of your phase 2 matrix; state the dimension of each and check the rank-nullity theorem.","Interpret five right null space basis vectors biologically; name the pathway each corresponds to.","Compute the left null space; identify every conserved pool and name the moiety it conserves.","Deliberately remove one exchange reaction; show how the left null space changes and explain why.","Repeat all four computations on the E. coli core model; compare the dimensions with your small network.","Perform an SVD of the core model's S; plot the singular value spectrum and interpret its shape.","Interpret the first three left and right singular vectors; write what each says about the network.","Compute elementary flux modes for a small subnetwork; compare the count with the null space dimension.","Write the degrees-of-freedom note: how many fluxes must be measured or constrained for your network, and which.","Read Lee §2.2.1-2.2.4; write the derivation from dS/dt to the flux balance formulation yourself.","Write notes/subspaces.md with every result and its biological interpretation.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":23,"p":4,"t":"Flux balance analysis: formulation, duality and interpretation","obj":["Formulate FBA from the dynamic mass balance and state every assumption in order of fragility","Set media, bounds and objectives correctly, with the right sign convention","Interpret shadow prices and reduced costs biologically","Cross-check model output against hand calculation"],"s":["Read Lee §2.2.1-2.2.3 and derive the FBA formulation from dS/dt on paper.","Read §2.2.4-2.2.6; write the formal LP statement with every constraint type named.","Read the Orth 2010 primer and log it under the paper protocol.","Re-pin the environment after twenty-two weeks of drift: rebuild it from the lock file, install COBRApy and a solver, run the bundled core model and confirm the published growth rate, then re-run the clean-clone test on every earlier notebook and record the solver version.","Work the COBRApy getting-started tutorials end to end; commit your worked notebook.","Compute maximum ATP yield per glucose aerobically and anaerobically; cross-check against your week 8 hand calculation and resolve the difference in writing.","Compute growth on glucose, acetate, succinate, glycerol and xylose; explain the ranking from stoichiometry before reading the output.","Read §2.2.7 on flux-concentration duality; write what the dual variables represent.","Extract shadow prices for ten metabolites at optimum; interpret the five largest.","Deliberately break the model: wrong exchange sign, missing medium component, unbounded ATP maintenance. Record each symptom.","Write notes/fba-assumptions.md: every assumption ranked by how likely it is to be wrong, with the evidence.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":24,"p":4,"t":"FBA extensions: variability, parsimony and thermodynamic consistency","obj":["Run and interpret FVA, pFBA, loopless FBA, MOMA and ROOM","Identify correlated reaction sets and explain what they mean","Diagnose thermodynamically infeasible loops and remove them","Choose the appropriate post-perturbation objective and justify it"],"s":["Run FVA on the core model at 100% and 90% of optimum under three media; tabulate which reactions are fixed and which vary.","Identify correlated reaction sets from the FVA output; explain three of them biologically.","Run pFBA; compare the flux distribution with plain FBA and write what the difference tells you.","Find a thermodynamically infeasible loop in a plain FBA solution; demonstrate it explicitly.","Run loopless FBA; show which fluxes changed and by how much, and explain the mechanism.","Read Lee §7.2.2-7.3; write why S·v = 0 permits loops at all.","Implement flux coupling analysis for a subnetwork; interpret the directional and full couplings you find.","Simulate a knockout with FBA, MOMA and ROOM; compare the three predictions and say which you believe and why.","Write the degeneracy note: for one reaction with a wide FVA range, how you would resolve it experimentally.","Code a small wrapper that runs your standard analysis suite on any model and emits a report.","Write notes/fba-extensions.md: when to use each method, with a decision rule rather than a list.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":25,"p":4,"t":"Gene deletions, essentiality and phenotypic phase planes","obj":["Run single and double deletion screens and build a confusion matrix against experimental data","Explain every false positive and false negative class mechanistically","Construct and read a phenotypic phase plane and a robustness analysis","Identify synthetic lethal pairs and interpret them"],"s":["Run a complete single gene deletion screen on the core model; record predicted essentiality for every gene.","Obtain experimental essentiality data and build the confusion matrix; compute precision and recall.","Take every false positive individually and write the mechanistic explanation; group them into classes.","Do the same for false negatives; note how many are threshold artefacts rather than biology.","Repeat the screen on a genome-scale model; compare the error rates and explain the difference in writing.","Run a double deletion screen on a subset; find synthetic lethal pairs and interpret three of them.","Run robustness analysis on oxygen uptake; plot growth against uptake and identify the regimes.","Construct a phenotypic phase plane for glucose and oxygen; identify every region and the line of optimality.","Explain the phase plane from stoichiometry alone, without reference to the computed output.","Write the validation note: what fraction of disagreements are model errors and what fraction are data errors.","Commit the full validation report with figures and the error classification.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":26,"p":4,"t":"Strain design algorithms and growth coupling","obj":["Formulate and solve a bilevel strain design problem","Explain growth coupling and construct a coupled design by hand","Compare the main design algorithm families and their assumptions","Criticise your own design against what the model cannot represent","Sit and mark Paper 3"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 8 §8.2.2 on pathway search (§8.2.2.1 stoichiometric, §8.2.2.2 graph-based, §8.2.2.3 ranking); write the difference between finding a pathway and designing a strain.","Produce a production envelope for your chosen product; read off the maximum yield and the coupled region.","Construct a growth-coupled design by hand: delete every competing sink and verify coupling stoichiometrically.","Formulate the bilevel problem on paper; explain why it is hard and how the standard reformulation works.","Run a knockout design search; propose a two or three deletion design and record the predicted yield.","Compare your hand design with the algorithm's; explain any difference and decide which you would build.","Run an overexpression target analysis; cross-check its suggestions against your week 11 anaplerosis reasoning.","Read Yim et al. closely and log it; note how model prediction, pathway search and strain building interacted.","Write the failure analysis: five reasons your design would underperform, each tied to a model limitation.","Consolidation: compress phase 4 into a six-page modelling reference.","Sit Paper 3 under exam conditions: three hours, open book and open computer.","Mark Paper 3 against the scheme; record the score and write what each lost mark revealed."]},{"n":27,"p":5,"t":"Metabolomics and intracellular measurement","obj":["Describe the full metabolomics workflow and the error introduced at each step","Explain why turnover time dictates quenching and sampling design","Compute an error budget for an intracellular concentration measurement","Judge whether a published metabolite concentration can support the claim built on it"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 9 §9.2-9.3; write every step from culture to data table, with the loss or bias at each.","Compute turnover times for ATP, PEP, G6P and glutamate from literature pools and fluxes; rank sampling difficulty.","Read Lee §9.3.1 on sample preparation; write why cold methanol quenching leaks, and what the differential method does about it.","Build the error budget: propagate sampling, quenching, extraction, dilution and instrument error for one metabolite.","Read Lee §9.2.2-9.2.3 on targeted against untargeted work and on standards; write the difference absolute quantification makes to a thermodynamic calculation.","Find a published intracellular concentration data set; note its method, its reported error, and what it omits.","Recompute ΔrG′ for five reactions of your pathway atlas using that measured data rather than assumed concentrations.","Compare those ΔrG′ with your phase 2 estimates; write what changed and whether any conclusion flips.","Read on compartment-specific measurement; write why a yeast cytosolic concentration is rarely trustworthy.","Write the diagnostic note: which three metabolite measurements you would demand before trusting a flux claim.","Commit notes/metabolomics.md with the error budget and the recomputed thermodynamics.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":28,"p":5,"t":"13C metabolic flux analysis I: principles and atom mapping","obj":["Explain how labelling resolves fluxes that extracellular rates cannot","Build an atom transition network and simulate labelling patterns","Define isotopomers, cumomers, EMUs and mass distribution vectors precisely","Correct measured data for natural isotope abundance"],"s":["Read Lee §3.1; write the three questions MFA answers that FBA cannot.","Work the chapter's toy example entirely by hand, reproducing every labelling pattern.","Read §3.2.2-3.2.3; write how labelling data becomes flux information, step by step.","Write out the atom transitions for every reaction in glycolysis and the PPP, by hand.","Read §3.2.8-3.2.10; explain why one metabolite's isotopomers are a rich information source.","Code the forward simulator: given fluxes and atom transitions, compute steady-state labelling patterns.","Verify your simulator against the hand-worked toy example; resolve every discrepancy.","Read §3.2.11 on bidirectional steps; add exchange fluxes to your model and show how they scramble label.","Read §3.5.7; implement natural abundance correction and test it on synthetic data.","Simulate 1-13C and U-13C glucose on your network; compare the information content qualitatively.","Write notes/mfa-principles.md with the atom maps and a worked explanation of the PPP split resolution.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":29,"p":5,"t":"13C metabolic flux analysis II: estimation, statistics and design","obj":["Estimate fluxes from labelling data by least squares with confidence intervals","Apply the chi-squared goodness-of-fit test and interpret its failure","Design a tracer experiment that maximises information about a target flux","Explain instationary MFA and when it is necessary"],"s":["Read Lee §3.2.5; write the least squares objective for labelling data including measurement weights.","Implement flux estimation on your toy network; fit to synthetic noisy data from a known flux vector.","Test recovery: can your estimator return the true fluxes, and how does error scale with noise?","Read §3.2.6; implement linearised confidence intervals and compare them with a bootstrap.","Read §3.3.1-3.3.2; implement the chi-squared test and deliberately induce a failure by omitting a reaction.","Interpret that failure correctly: show that it points to model structure rather than to noise.","Read §3.4.7; implement a simple experimental design loop comparing two tracers on target flux precision.","Add a third tracer mixture and show the information gain, or its absence, quantitatively.","Read §3.5.9 on INST-MFA; write what changes computationally and why autotrophs need it.","Combine your estimates with extracellular rate measurements; show how much the intervals tighten.","Write the critique note: five questions you will ask of any published MFA result from now on.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":30,"p":5,"t":"Kinetic models of metabolism","obj":["Choose between mechanistic and approximative rate laws with a defensible criterion","Build and integrate a kinetic model of a pathway to steady state","Estimate parameters and demonstrate sloppiness","Build and interpret an ensemble of models rather than one"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 5 §5.1-5.4; write the scope and the limits of a kinetic model in your own words.","Read §5.5; write a mechanistic reversible rate law for each reaction of a five-step pathway.","Impose thermodynamic consistency through the Haldane relation; show which parameters are then dependent.","Read §5.6; implement convenience kinetics for the same pathway and compare the two formulations.","Code the model: integrate to steady state; verify the steady state independently of the integrator.","Read §5.7; assemble parameters from BRENDA with their ranges, not point values.","Fit the remaining parameters to a target steady-state flux; show how many parameter sets fit equally well.","Demonstrate sloppiness: compute the parameter sensitivity spectrum and interpret its decades of spread.","Build an ensemble of one hundred parameter sets consistent with the data; propagate to a prediction interval.","Compare the ensemble prediction of a two-fold enzyme increase with the single best-fit prediction.","Write notes/kinetic-models.md on when a kinetic model earns its cost and when FBA is the honest choice.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":31,"p":5,"t":"Metabolic control analysis and proteome-constrained models","obj":["Compute flux and concentration control coefficients and verify the summation theorem","Distinguish elasticities from control coefficients and apply the connectivity theorem","Explain why single-gene overexpression plateaus","Add a proteome constraint and find the optimal enzyme allocation","Sit and mark Paper 4"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 6 §6.2.2 flux control coefficients and §6.2.4 the summation theorem; write every coefficient with its formal definition and its units.","Compute all flux control coefficients of your week 30 model numerically; verify the summation theorem.","Compute concentration control coefficients; interpret the largest ones biologically.","Compute elasticities for every enzyme; verify the connectivity theorem numerically.","Double each enzyme in turn; compare the actual flux change with the control coefficient prediction.","Show where the linear prediction fails; plot flux against enzyme level over a ten-fold range.","Demonstrate redistribution: recompute control coefficients after amplifying the controlling enzyme.","Read Lee Ch 4 §4.2-4.3; write the two approaches to proteome constraints and their data requirements.","Add an enzyme-cost constraint to your model; find the allocation maximising flux under a fixed budget.","Compare that optimal allocation with the MCA conclusion; write how the two analyses agree.","Read Lee §2.5-2.6; write what an ME model computes that an M model cannot, with two examples.","Sit and mark Paper 4 under exam conditions: three hours, open book."]},{"n":32,"p":6,"t":"DNA manipulation: PCR, cloning and assembly","obj":["Design a cloning strategy from backbone choice to verified transformant","Compare restriction, Gibson, Golden Gate, yeast assembly and synthesis on cost, time and failure mode","Design primers and verification steps for a real construct","Write a protocol another person could follow"],"s":["Read Berg Ch 5 §5.1-5.2; write the full PCR cycle with temperatures and the reason for each.","Design primers for three amplicons: compute Tm, check for hairpins and dimers, and state your design rules.","Read Alberts Ch 7; write the restriction-ligation workflow as a numbered protocol with timings.","Read Addgene's Plasmids 101 series; annotate one real plasmid map completely, naming every feature.","Study Gibson assembly; design the overlaps for a five-fragment assembly and check for repeat-driven misassembly.","Study Golden Gate; domesticate one gene in silico by removing internal type IIS sites without changing the protein.","Compare five assembly methods in a table: hands-on time, calendar time, cost, scar, multiplexing, failure mode.","Cost a real build: five genes into one vector, by each method, in days and in currency.","Design the verification cascade: what you check at each stage and what each check cannot catch.","Read Microbiology 12.1-12.2; write how transformation efficiency is measured and what limits it.","Write design/cloning-protocol.md as a protocol a technician could execute without asking you a question.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":33,"p":6,"t":"Vectors, expression systems and chassis preparation","obj":["Select origin, marker and copy number for a stated purpose and defend the choice","Explain plasmid instability and compute segregational loss over a seed train","Compare plasmid-based and chromosomally integrated expression quantitatively","Describe inducible systems, their leakiness and their cost at scale"],"s":["Read Shuler Ch 14 §14.3 and §14.5 on host-vector choice and plasmid design; tabulate five origins with copy number and compatibility from a vector catalogue, since Ch 14 tabulates host-vector systems and not origins.","Derive the segregational loss model; compute the producing fraction after 30 and after 60 generations.","Compute the number of generations in a real seed train from 1 mL to 50 m³; apply your loss model.","Read on markers; write the economics of antibiotic selection at production scale and three alternatives.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 8-9; write what tooling constraints mean for a non-model host and how they are worked around.","Compare plasmid and integrated expression: write the quantitative trade-off in copy number, stability and tunability.","Read Lee Ch 15 §15.2.3 on recombineering and CRISPR-Cas; list the integration methods and the effort each costs.","Study inducible systems; tabulate four with their dynamic range, leakiness and inducer cost per cubic metre.","Design decision: for your pathway, choose plasmid or integration, origin, marker and promoter, and defend every choice.","Compute the burden: estimate the proteome fraction and growth penalty your chosen configuration implies.","Extend the construction package with the vector design and its justification.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":34,"p":6,"t":"Tuning expression: promoters, RBS, copy number and burden","obj":["Predict the effect of promoter, RBS and copy number changes on pathway flux","Design a combinatorial tuning library sized to a realistic screen","Explain metabolic burden mechanistically and measure it correctly","Connect tuning to the enzyme-allocation optimum from week 31"],"s":["Read Berg Ch 31; draw the lac and trp operon logic as control diagrams with every feedback marked.","Read Alberts Ch 9 — Combinatorial Gene Control Is the Norm in Eucaryotes; write how eukaryotic regulation changes yeast pathway design.","Study RBS design; compute predicted initiation rates for five variants of one 5′ UTR and explain the ranking.","Read Lee Ch 13 §13.5; list every tuning mechanism used, with the fold-range each spans.","Read Ajikumar et al. closely and log it; extract the module definitions and the library size they screened.","Design your own library: modules, variants per module, total size, and the screen that could cover it.","Show the arithmetic: if the library exceeds your screening capacity, redesign it until it does not.","Connect to week 31: use your enzyme-allocation optimum to predict which module most needs variation.","Design the burden experiment: the inert-protein control, matched expression, and the measurements to take.","Estimate the growth penalty of your pathway at target flux from proteome fraction; state the assumptions.","Extend the construction package with the tuning library design and its screening plan.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":35,"p":6,"t":"Genome editing: recombineering, CRISPR and multiplexing","obj":["Describe lambda-Red recombineering and selection-counterselection in full","Explain CRISPR-Cas9 mechanism, guide design and repair outcomes","Choose between knockout, knockdown and promoter replacement for a given target","Write a complete editing plan for three designed interventions"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 10 §10.2.3 on CRISPR/Cas; write the full editing workflow including both repair outcomes.","Study lambda-Red; write the protocol for a markerless deletion with selection-counterselection, with timings.","Compare recombineering and CRISPR for the same deletion: steps, days, efficiency, screening burden.","Design guides for three targets; check PAM availability, predicted efficiency and off-targets.","Design the donor DNA for each: homology arm length, scar, and how you will verify the edit.","Read on CRISPRi; explain why knockdown beats knockout for an essential gene, with an example from week 25.","Take one essential gene from your deletion screen; design a graded knockdown series for it.","Read Lee §12.2.2 and §15.2.3; write why a method that works in E. coli may not transfer.","Read on MAGE and multiplex editing; compute the library diversity achievable in a stated number of cycles.","Write the full editing plan for your three designed interventions, with expected efficiencies and fallbacks.","Read Methods §11 and add the biosafety section to your editing plan: containment level, approval route, and any dual-use consideration with its mitigation. Then review the whole plan against your construction package and fix every mismatch.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":36,"p":6,"t":"Dynamic regulation and biosensors","obj":["Design a metabolite-responsive control loop for a pathway","Explain how transcription-factor and riboswitch biosensors work and how they are characterised","Analyse a genetic control loop with the tools you already use for process control","Use a biosensor to convert a screen into a selection"],"s":["Read Lee §11.2.4 and §18.2.5.3; list every implemented architecture with the quantity it senses. Note that neither chapter has a dynamic-regulation section — both are organised by product.","Read Protein Engineering Ch 2; write how a biosensor turns a chemical phenotype into a selectable one.","Characterise a sensor on paper: define dynamic range, operating point and response time, and how each is measured.","Design the loop: choose the sensed metabolite, the actuated step, and the sign of the feedback.","Model it: add the sensor and the regulated promoter to your week 30 kinetic model.","Analyse stability and gain; find the parameter region where the loop oscillates or fails to settle.","Compare the controlled and uncontrolled pathway under a substrate pulse; quantify the benefit.","Design the quorum-sensing alternative: growth phase switching without an inducer, with its timing logic.","Write the selection design: how your sensor converts product titre into a growth or fluorescence advantage.","State the failure modes: sensor escape, crosstalk, mutation of the reporter, and the countermeasure for each.","Commit design/dynamic-control.md with the model, the analysis and the figures.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":37,"p":6,"t":"Enzyme engineering: rational design and directed evolution","obj":["Compare rational design, directed evolution and semi-rational approaches on information requirement","Compute library size against screening capacity and choose a strategy that fits","Design a complete evolution campaign with rounds, diversity and stopping criterion","Explain the stability-activity trade-off and how to buffer it"],"s":["Read Protein Engineering Ch 5 Table 5.1; compute library sizes for saturation at 3, 5, 8 and 10 positions.","Tabulate screening throughput by format and match each library size to the formats that can cover it.","Study reduced codon sets in §5.2.1; compute the saving from NDT against NNK and state the eight amino acids NDT omits.","Read Ch 1 on continuous evolution; write its throughput, its bias, and what it cannot select for.","Read Ch 11 §11.1; write how an industrial campaign differs from an academic one in target, metric and stopping rule.","Identify the weakest enzyme in your pathway from your week 31 control analysis; state the property to improve.","Design the campaign: library strategy, diversity, screen, rounds, expected gain per round.","Add the stability buffer: which stabilising mutations or approach you would apply first, and why.","Read Ch 10 §10.2-10.3.1 if time allows; write when unnatural amino acids are justified and the infrastructure they require.","Write the stopping criterion as a measurable result and a date, with the alternative if it is not met.","Commit design/evolution-campaign.md with every arithmetic step shown.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":38,"p":6,"t":"Data-driven protein design and high-throughput screening","obj":["Explain what sequence and structure models predict, and the data each needs","Design a machine-learning-guided campaign with its train-test discipline","Judge a published ML protein design claim critically","Match screening technology to library size and to the property being measured"],"s":["Read Protein Engineering Ch 6 §6.3-6.4; write what each model class predicts and what data it was trained on.","Write the epistasis problem: why a model trained on single mutants fails on combinations, with an example.","Run ESM-1v or ESM-2 on your dossier enzyme; extract per-position conservation and record the model and checkpoint used.","Compare that conservation with the structural active site you mapped in week 3; note agreements and conflicts.","Design an active learning loop: batch size, acquisition strategy, and the stopping rule.","Specify the train-test discipline that would make your result credible, including held-out positions.","Read Lee §9.5.1; match three screening technologies to the properties they can actually measure, using its throughput figures.","Design the screen for your campaign: technology, throughput, false positive rate, and the confirmation assay.","Read Lee §2.6.3; write how structural information is being integrated into genome-scale models.","Critique one published ML-guided design paper; write the three claims it does not support.","Extend the evolution campaign with the ML-guided variant and compare expected cost per improvement.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":39,"p":6,"t":"Omics, the design-build-test-learn cycle and adaptive evolution","obj":["Describe how each omics layer is produced and what it can and cannot support","Integrate a real public omics data set with a genome-scale model","Design a DBTL cycle with an explicit, falsifiable learning step","Design an adaptive laboratory evolution experiment and the analysis that follows it","Sit and mark Paper 5"],"s":["Take two conditions from PRECISE-1K via iModulonDB, or an equivalent single-protocol compendium for your host; record the full provenance including the reference genome accession.","Perform differential expression yourself; justify the normalisation and the significance threshold you chose.","Map the significant genes onto model reactions through the GPRs, handling complexes and isozymes correctly.","Constrain the model with the expression data by one standard integration method.","Compare predictions with and without the constraint; write what improved, what degraded, and why.","Read Lee §2.6.2; write what independent component analysis recovers that differential expression does not.","Read Palsson §26.1-26.3; design a selection scheme for your product phenotype and predict what will break first.","Design the post-ALE analysis per §26.2: sequencing including indels of 1-80 bp, causal mutation identification, reconstruction in the parent, and the calendar it costs.","Write the DBTL plan: for your strain, what you measure after round one and what each result would change.","Make the learning step falsifiable: state in advance which model assumption each outcome would reject.","Sit Paper 5 under exam conditions: three hours, open book and open computer.","Mark Paper 5 against the scheme; record the score and what each lost mark revealed."]},{"n":40,"p":7,"t":"Escherichia coli as a platform","obj":["Recount the major E. coli engineering campaigns with their numbers","Explain acetate overflow and the full set of strategies against it","Describe the tool stack available in E. coli and what it costs","Judge when E. coli is the wrong choice"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 11 §11.2-11.3; log it under the paper protocol as a review. Note it is a product survey, not a central-metabolism chapter.","Extract every quantitative claim in the chapter into a table: product, titre, rate, yield, strain, conditions.","Read Basan et al. and Vemuri et al.; write all four mechanistic causes of acetate overflow and the strategy that addresses each. Ch 11 mentions acetate six times and never mechanistically.","Compute the yield penalty of acetate at a stated carbon flux; show it in g/g and in money per tonne.","Read Shuler §14.2 and §14.3.2 with Lee §11.4.1; write what differs when the product is the protein itself.","Read Lee §2.4; write the sequence of E. coli models and what each added in scope.","Reread Yim et al.; write three criticisms you could not have made in week 26.","Build the E. coli page of the host matrix: physiology, tools, products, strengths, failure modes.","Take one product from the table and recompute its reported yield as a fraction of theoretical; comment.","Log a second E. coli paper in full under the protocol.","Write the judgement note: three situations in which you would not choose E. coli, with reasons. Then begin the monthly practice from Methods §12: present one logged paper aloud for ten minutes at a whiteboard, recorded, and answer the three hardest questions you can raise against it.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":41,"p":7,"t":"Yeasts: Saccharomyces and the non-conventional species","obj":["Compare S. cerevisiae with E. coli on every property that decides host choice","Explain the Crabtree effect and its consequences for process design","Describe compartmentalised pathway engineering and its targeting requirements","Say what Yarrowia, Pichia and Kluyveromyces each offer"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 18 §18.1 with Shuler Ch 5 §5.1; tabulate the differences from E. coli that affect design. Ch 18 has no physiology or tools section — it is organised by product.","Read Shuler §5.1 and the bakers' yeast appendix; compute the respiratory capacity limit and the fed-batch feed rate it implies.","Read Alberts Ch 12, and Ch 13 for the vacuole; list each compartment with its targeting signal and a pathway placed there. Cite the prose, not Table 12-3, which does not extract from this PDF.","Write the compartment decision for your own pathway: where you would put it in yeast, and why.","Read Lee §18.2.5.3 on sesquiterpenes; write why yeast handles membrane-bound P450 chemistry better than E. coli.","Read Ro et al. and log it; then find and read the later process papers on the same product.","Write the artemisinin case note: why a scientific success took a decade to reach the market, in specifics.","Read Lee Ch 19 §19.4 on triacylglycerol, with §19.4.2 push-and-pull; write what lipid-body storage offers a hydrophobic product.","Compare Pichia and Yarrowia for your product class; state which you would pick and what would change it.","Add the yeast pages to the host matrix, with numbers from the chapters.","Log one further yeast paper in full under the protocol.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":42,"p":7,"t":"Corynebacterium, Bacillus and Pseudomonas","obj":["Explain why C. glutamicum dominates amino acid production","Describe Bacillus secretion and what it is used for","Explain Pseudomonas robustness and its Entner-Doudoroff metabolism","Match each host to the product classes it genuinely suits"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 12 §12.2 and §12.4.1.2, where the anaplerotic material sits; connect it to your week 11 node map. There is no physiology section.","Read Lee §12.4.1.2; tabulate each strain generation with its interventions and yield.","Compute lysine's theoretical yield from glucose yourself; compare with the best published strain.","Read Lee Ch 13 §13.2 and §13.7; write what secretion capacity is worth and how it is measured.","Write why sporulation must be disabled in a production strain and what that costs.","Read Lee Ch 14 §14.2.1-14.2.2 on P. putida and its unique core metabolism; write how the ED pathway changes its NADPH availability.","Compute the NADPH advantage quantitatively and name a product class that benefits.","Read Lee §14.3.3-14.3.4 on the oxygen-dependent lifestyle and aromatics; write the solvent-tolerance mechanisms and what they offer for a toxic product.","Add all three hosts to the matrix with documented products and numbers.","Product-host matching: build a table mapping eight product classes to their best-fit host, with reasons.","Log one paper from these hosts in full under the protocol.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":43,"p":7,"t":"Lactic acid bacteria and food-grade hosts","obj":["Explain why food-grade status changes the host calculus","Describe LAB metabolism, nutritional requirements and their cost consequences","Describe the genetic tools available in lactobacilli and their limits","Judge when a food-grade host is justified despite its costs"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 15 §15.1.1-15.1.2 on features, phylogeny and the metabolism of LAB; connect it to your week 13 fermentation ledger.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 1; write what genome reduction means for medium cost and for engineering.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 4; recompute lactate yields and compare with the published strains.","Cost the medium: for a complex LAB medium, compute the raw material cost per kilogram of lactate.","Compare that with the same product in E. coli on defined medium; write the trade-off explicitly.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 3; write how transporter repertoire decides substrate scope for prebiotics.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 9; tabulate the expression systems available and their dynamic range.","Read Lactobacillus Ch 5-6; write what the surface layer offers for display or delivery applications.","Write the regulatory note: what GRAS and QPS status actually mean for an engineered strain's path to market.","Add LAB to the host matrix, including the regulatory column that other hosts lack.","Log one LAB paper in full under the protocol.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":44,"p":7,"t":"Clostridia, actinomycetes, fungi and photosynthetic hosts","obj":["Explain what each specialist host uniquely offers","Describe solventogenesis and gas fermentation","Describe polyketide and non-ribosomal peptide biosynthesis and why actinomycetes host it","State honestly where photosynthetic production stands on rate and cost"],"s":["Read Lee Ch 16 §16.1-16.2; write the ABE fermentation stoichiometry and its yield ceiling, which the chapter does not supply — derive it from the week 13 fermentation balances.","Compute the energetics of acetogenesis from CO and H₂ from the week 8 and week 13 balances; no assigned chapter covers gas fermentation.","Read Lee Ch 17 §17.3 on natural product biosynthetic pathways; write how a polyketide synthase assembles a product.","Write why heterologous expression of a biosynthetic gene cluster is hard, with four specific reasons.","Read Lee Ch 20 §20.7 on controlled macromorphologies with Doran §7.7-7.8; write how morphology affects broth rheology and therefore oxygen transfer.","Compute the oxygen transfer penalty of a viscous fungal broth relative to a bacterial one.","Read Lee Ch 21; extract the reported areal and volumetric productivities for photosynthetic production.","Compare those numbers against a heterotrophic fermentation on the same basis; write the honest conclusion.","Read Lee Ch 22 §22.5 bottlenecks in environmental biocatalysts and §22.10 conclusions; write where the economics genuinely closes today.","Add all four host classes to the matrix with their productivity numbers.","Write the specialist-host note: for each, the one product class that justifies its difficulty.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":45,"p":7,"t":"Product classes I: bulk chemicals, organic acids and alcohols","obj":["Compute the theoretical yield and the economic ceiling for a bulk product","Explain the recurring design patterns for organic acids and alcohols","Explain acid tolerance and the trade-off between fermentation pH and downstream cost","Judge a published bulk-chemical claim against commercial thresholds"],"s":["Compute theoretical yields for ethanol, butanol, isobutanol, lactate, succinate and 3-HP from glucose.","For each, identify whether carbon or electrons bind, and whether CO₂ fixation could raise the ceiling.","Read Lee §11.2.1-11.2.2; tabulate reported titre, rate and yield for three alcohol strains.","Read Lee §11.3.1 and §12.4.2; do the same for three acid strains, and express each as a fraction of theoretical.","Write the acid tolerance note: the mechanism of weak-acid inhibition, connected to your week 2 derivation.","Compute the downstream consequence of neutralised fermentation: base consumption and salt per tonne of product.","Read Stanbury Ch 12, named sections \"media\", \"aeration and agitation\", \"batch-process cycle times\" and \"recovery costs\"; build the cost skeleton for one bulk product.","Compute the substrate cost fraction at the published yield; judge viability against the selling price.","Derive the yield a viable process would require; compare with the theoretical maximum and comment.","Write the dossier for one bulk product: route, host, numbers, economics, and what stopped it scaling.","Log one bulk-chemical paper in full under the protocol.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":46,"p":7,"t":"Product classes II: amino acids, natural products and proteins","obj":["Explain the distinct economics of high-value products","Describe terpenoid, polyketide and flavonoid production and their characteristic bottlenecks","Explain what changes when the product is a protein","Synthesise the whole phase into a defended product and host choice"],"s":["Write the economics of a high-value product: why substrate cost becomes negligible and purification dominates.","Read Lee §18.2.5.3; write both precursor routes with their ATP, NADPH and carbon demand.","Compute the theoretical yield of a C15 terpenoid by each route and compare.","Reread Ajikumar et al.; write the module balance in terms of your week 31 control analysis.","Read Lee §17.5.1-17.5.2 on host selection and BGC cloning; write the four obstacles to heterologous cluster expression.","Write the malonyl-CoA note: why flavonoid and polyketide titres are so often precursor-limited, with numbers.","Read Shuler Ch 11 §11.4 on soluble products and §11.5 finishing steps; write what changes in design when the protein is the product.","Compare intracellular, periplasmic and secreted production on downstream cost.","Write the dossier for one high-value product with its characteristic bottleneck and the countermeasure.","Phase synthesis: choose your product and host for the capstone, and defend both with numbers.","Write the literature synthesis: what the forty logged papers collectively say about where the field is stuck, with one page per month of phase 7 already written and now assembled.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":47,"p":8,"t":"Media, sterilisation and inoculum development","obj":["Design and cost a production medium for a stated strain and target","Compute a sterilisation cycle by the del factor method, including heat-up and cool-down","Design a seed train from vial to production vessel with its timing and risk","State the contamination risk you accept and why"],"s":["Read Stanbury Ch 4 — Media for Industrial Fermentations (printed p93), named sections \"typical media\" and \"the addition of precursors and metabolic regulators to media\"; design a defined medium for your strain with every component justified.","Compute the medium cost per kilogram of product at your expected yield, with sourced raw material prices.","Repeat with a complex medium; compare cost and state which you would use and under what conditions.","Read Stanbury Ch 4 on carbon, nitrogen and energy sources; compare glucose, sucrose and a hydrolysate on cost, variability and inhibitor content.","Read Stanbury Ch 5 — Sterilization (printed p123), named sections \"design of batch sterilization processes\", \"design of continuous sterilization processes\" and \"sterilization of the fermenter\"; derive the thermal death kinetics and the del factor. This book numbers no sections.","Compute a batch sterilisation cycle including the heat-up and cool-down contributions; report the total del.","Compute the nutrient destruction over that cycle; decide what must be sterilised separately.","Compare with continuous sterilisation; compute the difference in medium damage and in capital requirement.","Read Stanbury Ch 6; design the seed train from vial to your production volume, with ratios and timings.","Compute the number of generations in that train; apply your week 33 plasmid stability model to it.","Write process/media-and-sterilisation.md with every calculation shown.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":48,"p":8,"t":"Heterogeneous reaction, immobilisation and reactor choice","obj":["Derive and solve the steady-state diffusion-reaction balance in a spherical catalyst particle with Michaelis-Menten kinetics","Compute the Thiele modulus and effectiveness factor, and decide the controlling regime from an observable criterion","Compare immobilisation chemistries on activity retention, stability, leaching and cost","Select a reactor configuration for an immobilised system and defend it against the free-catalyst alternative"],"s":["Read Doran Ch 12 §12.1-12.2; write why a heterogeneous biological catalyst is a diffusion problem before it is a kinetic one.","Derive the steady-state shell balance for a sphere with first-order reaction by hand; solve it analytically and plot the internal profile.","Read §12.3; derive the Thiele modulus and the effectiveness factor, and reproduce the classical curve yourself rather than copying it.","Extend to Michaelis-Menten kinetics numerically; show how the effectiveness factor curve shifts as the substrate moves from first-order to zero-order regime.","Read §12.4; apply the Weisz-Prater criterion to a case where you do not know the intrinsic kinetics, and state what it can and cannot decide.","Estimate the effective diffusivity in a gel bead: a liquid correlation from Plawsky Ch 3, then porosity and tortuosity corrections, with the uncertainty carried through.","Read §12.5-12.8; add external film resistance from a liquid-solid correlation and find the conditions under which it dominates internal resistance.","Apparent kinetics exercise: simulate a substrate titration on a diffusion-limited bead and fit Michaelis-Menten to the result; report the apparent KM and explain the discrepancy.","Tabulate stirred, packed bed, fluidised bed and membrane reactors on mass transfer, pressure drop, retention and attrition. Doran Ch 13 is the chapter, but only Ch 7, 9, 11 and 12 are verified in this handoff — run `toc.py outline` on the PDF for its sections before citing one.","Read Lee §20.7 with Doran §12.2-12.4; compute the oxygen penetration depth into a fungal pellet and the radius at which the core goes anaerobic.","Design session: for a stated conversion and feed concentration, size bead radius, catalyst loading and reactor volume, and show the trade-off against pressure drop and retention.","Weekly review and recall set; write the comparison against free catalyst on productivity, catalyst cost per kilogram and operational stability."]},{"n":49,"p":8,"t":"Fermenter design, aeration and mixing","obj":["Size a vessel for a stated oxygen demand and check every constraint","Compute kLa from power input and gas flow, and know the correlation's limits","Identify the scale-up constraints that conflict and choose which to hold constant","Design the vessel for your capstone process"],"s":["Read Stanbury Ch 7 — Design of a Fermenter (printed p167), named sections \"basic functions of a fermenter\", \"fermenter body construction\" and \"other fermentation vessels\"; specify geometry, impellers and baffles for your process.","Read Ch 9 §9.1-9.4; derive the gassed power draw and compute it for your vessel.","Compute kLa from a standard correlation; state the correlation's basis and its uncertainty.","Check it against your week 12 OUR requirement; iterate the design until the balance closes.","Compute mixing time and circulation time at your scale; compare with the strain's metabolic response time.","Compute heat generation from the oxygen uptake rate and size the cooling duty.","Check shear: compute tip speed and compare with tolerance for your host class.","Read Shuler §10.2.4 and Table 10.2; compute what each of four scale-up criteria implies for power at 50 m³.","Show the conflict numerically: tabulate what holding each criterion constant does to the others.","Choose and defend one criterion for your process; state what you accept degrading.","Write process/vessel-design.md with the full calculation chain from strain OUR to vessel specification.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":50,"p":8,"t":"Instrumentation, control and feeding strategies","obj":["Specify the instrumentation and control scheme for a production fermentation","Design a feeding strategy and justify its setpoint from physiology","Use off-gas and soft sensors as real-time diagnostics","Design the scale-down experiment that tests your strain against large-scale gradients"],"s":["Read Stanbury Ch 8 — Instrumentation and Control (printed p215); tabulate every sensor with what it measures and its failure mode.","Design the DO control cascade: agitation, aeration, enrichment, pressure, in priority order with setpoints.","Derive the exponential feed profile for a target specific growth rate; compute the feed rate over time.","Justify the growth rate setpoint from your phase 3 overflow data; state the margin you leave and why.","Design the DO-stat alternative; write its advantage and its instability risk.","Build the off-gas soft sensor: from CO₂ and O₂ in the exhaust, compute OUR, CER and RQ in real time.","Show what each RQ deviation would indicate; build the diagnostic table.","Read Shuler §10.2.4 and §10.2.5 on scale-up and scale-down; design the two-compartment scale-down experiment for your process.","Specify the cycling timescale from your week 49 mixing time; state what you would measure and expect.","Write the control philosophy document: every loop, setpoint, alarm and the response to each alarm.","Extend the process package with instrumentation, control and the feeding strategy.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":51,"p":8,"t":"Downstream processing","obj":["Select a recovery and purification train from physical properties and defend each unit","Compute yields and costs stage by stage and find the dominant term","Explain in situ product removal as both a toxicity and a thermodynamic intervention","Decide intracellular versus secreted production on downstream grounds"],"s":["Read Stanbury Ch 10 — The Recovery and Purification of Fermentation Products (printed p277); select and size the cell separation for your broth.","Read Stanbury Ch 10 on primary recovery and solvent recovery; select the route from your product's solubility, volatility and charge.","Read Shuler Ch 11 §11.4 soluble products (§11.4.1-11.4.11, extraction through electrodialysis) and §11.5 finishing steps; specify the full train with a stated purity target.","Compute the stage-by-stage yield; show the overall recovery and identify the worst stage.","Compute the energy duty of the concentration steps using the thermodynamics you already know.","Compare intracellular and secreted production: compute the extra cost of disruption and clarification.","Read Lee §7.2.2-7.3; connect ISPR to the driving-force argument from week 12. Ch 7 is thermodynamics of pathways and says nothing about in-situ product removal.","Design the ISPR option for your product; compute the titre increase it could allow and its cost.","Cost the whole train per kilogram of product; identify the dominant term.","Write the sensitivity: how downstream cost changes with titre at 10, 25, 50 and 100 g/L.","Extend the process package with the downstream train and its costing.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":52,"p":8,"t":"Techno-economics and life-cycle assessment","obj":["Build a techno-economic model from equipment sizing to cost of goods","Rank titre, rate and yield by their elasticity on cost","Derive a strain specification from the economics","Perform a screening life-cycle assessment and state its limits honestly"],"s":["Read Stanbury Ch 12 — Fermentation Economics, named sections \"media\", \"aeration and agitation\", \"batch-process cycle times\", \"recovery costs\", \"effluent treatment\"; list every cost line for your process with its basis. Costs are as printed in 1999 — the ratios travel, the absolute figures do not.","Size and cost the major equipment; apply installation factors and state the estimate's accuracy class.","Build the OPEX model: raw materials, utilities, labour, waste treatment, capital charge.","Compute cost of goods per kilogram; compare with the market price you identified in phase 7.","Run the sensitivity: vary titre, rate and yield by ±30% and rank their effect on cost.","Compute the elasticity of cost to each parameter; write which strain work is economically justified.","Derive the strain specification: minimum titre, rate and yield the economics requires, with the arithmetic.","Compare that specification against what your week 26 design predicts; state the gap explicitly.","Run a Monte Carlo on the three most uncertain inputs; report the cost distribution rather than a point.","Perform a screening LCA: boundary, functional unit, and the two dominant impact contributors.","Write the honest paragraph: what this analysis cannot tell you, and what data would be needed.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":53,"p":8,"t":"Capstone I: the strain","obj":["Assemble a complete strain design for your chosen product and host","Show the full chain from stoichiometry through thermodynamics to flux model to genotype","State the expected performance against the specification from week 52","Name every unknown and the experiment that would resolve it"],"s":["Write the target justification: market size, price, incumbent route, and why biology could win.","Assemble the pathway with every reaction balanced and every ΔrG′ stated, from artifacts 5 and 6.","Re-run the MDF analysis on the final route; confirm feasibility at realistic concentrations.","Re-run the flux model with the final host and medium; report predicted yield and growth-coupled region.","Finalise the genotype: every deletion, overexpression and insertion, each justified stoichiometrically.","Re-run the deletion validation to check none of your targets is predicted essential in error.","Assemble the construction plan from artifacts 19 and 20 into one coherent build sequence with timings.","Estimate the expected titre, rate and yield with a stated uncertainty, not a single number.","Compare against the week 52 specification; state the gap and what would close it.","Build the risk register: every risk, its likelihood, its impact and your mitigation.","Write the unknowns section: what cannot be known before experiments, and the first experiment for each.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":54,"p":8,"t":"Capstone II: the process and the economics","obj":["Integrate the process design and the economics with the strain design","Show the feedback from economics back into the strain specification","Produce a dossier that a stranger can follow and check","Make every notebook in the repository run from a clean environment"],"s":["Integrate the process design with the final strain performance; update every number that changed.","Close the overall mass balance across the whole process; report carbon recovery end to end.","Close the energy balance; report utility demand per kilogram of product.","Recompute cost of goods with the final numbers; update the sensitivity table.","Write the feedback section: the single strain parameter the economics most demands, with the arithmetic.","Write the executive first page: problem, approach, result, cost, and the three main uncertainties.","Assemble the full dossier; check every figure is referenced and every number traceable to a calculation.","Reproducibility pass one: fresh clone, fresh environment, restart and run every notebook; fix what breaks.","Reproducibility pass two: have every data file's provenance recorded, and pin every dependency version.","Rewrite every notebook README: what it shows, what it does not show, how to run it.","Read the whole dossier aloud; cut every sentence that asserts rather than demonstrates.","Weekly review and recall set."]},{"n":55,"p":8,"t":"The application: proposal, portfolio and supervisors","obj":["Write a three-page proposal with a falsifiable hypothesis and a first experiment","Present the portfolio so a busy academic can assess it quickly","Build and contact a shortlist of groups with a specific proposition","Sit and mark Paper 6, and write an honest assessment of what remains weak"],"s":["Reread every week notebook; extract every question you wrote and still cannot answer.","Group those questions; choose the one that is both answerable in three years and genuinely unresolved.","Draft the proposal: problem and gap in one page, hypothesis in one sentence, three aims with methods.","Sharpen aim 1 until it names the first experiment, the measurement, and both possible outcomes.","Write the risk and alternative for each aim, and add the biosafety and dual-use paragraph from Methods §11 if your target has any such dimension. Delete any aim that has no credible alternative route.","Rewrite the repository README as a portfolio index: what each artifact shows and does not show.","Build the shortlist: ten groups, each with a recent paper read and a one-sentence fit argument.","Write the outreach emails; make each one specific to an unresolved result in that group's work.","Prepare the interview: for every artifact, the one figure and the one thing you would do differently.","Write the honest assessment: what you still cannot do, and your plan for it in the first six months.","Sit Paper 6 under exam conditions: three and a half hours, open book.","Mark Paper 6, record the score, and send the emails."]}]};
